from document_renderer import *
verses=[('Care greeting • infants','Here I am, and here are you.\nI will tell you what I do.'),('Putting away • toddlers','One cloth resting, one cloth here.\nNow the place for play is clear.'),('Walking • toddlers and older','Slow feet, quick feet, choose your way.\nWe have room to move today.'),('Table care • ages three to six','A place for you, a place for me.\nWho else is coming? Let us see.'),('Color • ages three to six','A line can wander, pause and bend.\nI choose the place where it will end.'),('Outdoor pause • any age with adaptation','A little breeze, a changing sky.\nWe stop a moment, you and I.'),('Departure • any age','Our time today is nearly through.\nOne more goodbye, and home with you.')]
stories=[
('The cloth by the door','Mina found a clean cloth beside the door. A little water had dripped from the boots. “That spot is wet,” said the adult. Mina pressed the cloth against the floor. The dark wet patch became smaller. She turned the cloth and pressed again. Then she put the cloth in the wash basket. At the door, another child was taking off boots. Mina pointed to the dry place. “Here,” she said. There was room to come inside.','Useful care; sequence; welcoming another child.','A real cloth for a child-led retelling, kept out of infant areas.'),
('A place for one more','Four friends made a pretend house. They put four wide paper squares around a box table. Then Eli arrived. “May I visit?” Eli asked with a picture card. The friends looked at the table. Nia moved her square a little. Tom moved his too. Eli chose another square and placed it beside them. Their table was still small, but their circle had grown. They played that a meal was ready. After a while, Eli chose to leave. The friends kept a place for the next visitor.','Accessible communication; counting real places; invitation without forced participation.','Large paper shapes; ask children how they would arrange an accessible play space.'),
('The path after rain','After rain, Jun and the adult walked their familiar path. The low place held a puddle. “We can look from here,” said the adult. A leaf floated across the water. Jun watched until it rested at the edge. They chose the dry accessible route around the puddle. At the tree, Jun noticed dark bark and shining drops. On the way back, they looked once more. The leaf had turned. “It changed,” Jun said. Tomorrow they could look again.','Local observation; a safe route; noticing change.','Adapt rain to an actual local weather event; no need to enter water.'),
('The bridge that needed room','Ari built a bridge from large blocks. A wide toy vehicle could not pass beneath it. Ari tried turning the vehicle, but it still stopped. Bea brought another block. Together they made the supports taller. The vehicle passed. Then Ari noticed the bridge wobbling. They stopped the game and rebuilt the base on the floor. This time the bridge stood firmly. “A wider opening and a steady bottom,” Ari said. Bea drove slowly through, and then it was Ari’s turn.','Revision; space and comparison; cooperation.','Large age-safe blocks and vehicles; build at a safe height, no climbing.'),
('A message for the garden','The class had a small planter. One morning its soil needed checking. Sol helped the adult follow the plant-care routine. Afterward Sol wanted everyone to know the plant had been cared for. The adult asked, “What could our sign say?” Sol said, “Checked today.” The adult wrote the words and read them aloud. Sol added a picture and the date with help. They put the sign where the group could see it. Later, another child read the message with the adult. Tomorrow the plant would need another look, not a guess.','Meaningful print; purposeful care; explicit adult reading support.','Use an approved non-toxic plant; adults determine care and safe materials.'),
('The book of the windy corner','Leah liked the windy corner of the yard. She told the adult, “My hat nearly flew.” The adult wrote her words, reading them back. Leah added a picture. On another page she drew a sheltered place. She asked how to write “hat.” The adult helped her listen to the sounds and connect them with letters at a suitable level. Together they read the little book. Leah changed one picture, then shared it with a friend who chose to listen. The windy corner had become a story they could return to.','Authorship; revision; timely direct literacy teaching.','Use a real local experience; no loose hats with unsafe ties or cords.')]
PAGES=[page('Original stories and verses',['p','Original language for this curriculum. These are invitations, not required scripts. Choose a comfortable home language, signs, pictures or communication device. Never use a story to frighten, shame or demand obedience.'],['p','Infants: use a gentle phrase in actual responsive care and stop with cues. Toddlers: tell one or two real actions, perhaps “The cloth is wet. We put it in the basket.” The six longer stories are starting points for ages three to six, shortened or extended for individual interest.'],['p','For older children, tell a familiar story again over several days if welcomed. Retelling may be movement, drawing, dictation, play or speech. A quiet observer can participate without performing.'])]
PAGES.append(page('Seven optional verses',*sum(([['h',t],['p',v]] for t,v in verses),[])))
for title,story,purpose,prop in stories:PAGES.append(page(title,['p','Original short story • ages three to six, adapted to interest'],['p',story],['h','Teacher use'],['p',purpose],['p',prop],['p','Tell naturally once, pause and follow the children’s response. Do not quiz for a moral. On another day, offer materials for an optional retelling or invite a child’s different ending.']))
export_book('Waldorf_Original_Stories_and_Verses',PAGES)
(R/'Original_Stories_and_Verses.md').write_text('\n\n'.join(['# Original stories and verses']+['## '+p['title']+'\n\n'+'\n\n'.join(x[1] for x in p['blocks']) for p in PAGES])+'\n')
